<http://model.geneontology.org/6ab067da00001836> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
                                                  owl:versionIRI <http://model.geneontology.org/6ab067da00001836> ;
                                                  <http://geneontology.org/lego/conforms_to_gpad> "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
                                                  <http://geneontology.org/lego/modelstate> "development" ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/contributor> "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7646-0052"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/date> "2026-10-01"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/title> "Collagen fibril organization via TIP39-PTH2R-CREB1-DCN (Human)"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/pav/providedBy> "http://geneontology.org"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  rdfs:comment "Curated with GO AI HUB. Human TIP39 activates PTH2R on dermal fibroblasts; PTH2R signaling leads (mechanism unknown) to CREB1 activation, CREB1 activates DCN (decorin) transcription, and decorin acts in collagen fibril organization; part of TIP39-PTH2R positive regulation of collagen fibril organization in wound healing. Companion to gomodel:6ab067da00001778 (keratinocyte arm)."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "Evidence basis: ligand-receptor PMID:34353904 (human cryo-EM). PTH2R in human fibroblasts: siRNA lowers ECM genes incl. DCN (PMID:28454729). TIP39-induced CREB phosphorylation/nuclear translocation, decorin induction, Tip39-/- wound delay and Pth2r-/- collagen fibril defects are mouse data (PMID:28454729) and are transferred by ISS (GO_REF:0000024, with MGI). CREB binding to the human DCN promoter: PMID:10966485 (abstract only). Decorin in collagen fibril organization: Dcn-null mice, PMID:9024701 (ISS). The kinase linking PTH2R to CREB is not identified (authors suggest Gs-AC-cAMP); hence causally upstream of, positive effect. pCREB detection is antibody-based."^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <https://w3id.org/biolink/vocab/in_taxon> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_9606> .

